<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartolomeu Dias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1500">1500</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartolomeu Dias was a Portuguese navigator who in 1488 became the first European known to sail around the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. His voyage proved that the Atlantic connected with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. It opened the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias.jpg" alt="Bartolomeu Dias" width="189" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RedCoat (RedCoat10); derivative work by Biser Todorov · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolomeu_Dias,_South_Africa_House_(cut).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dias came from a family with a maritime background. One of his ancestors, Dinis Dias e Fernandes, explored the African coast in the 1440s. Tracing his own biography is complicated by the existence of several Portuguese seafarers of the same name. In 1481 he accompanied an expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-de-azambuja">Diogo de Azambuja</a> to build the fortress and trading post of São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, and indirect evidence suggests he may have joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a>'s first expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. On 10 October 1486 King John II of Portugal granted him an annuity of 6,000 reis for services to come.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Probably in July or August 1487 Dias left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> with three ships, to continue the exploration advanced by Cão and to look for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prester-john">Prester John</a>, a Christian ruler believed to reign somewhere beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He carried stone pillars, padrões, to mark landfalls, and six Africans kidnapped by Cão and taught Portuguese, whom he meant to leave along the coast. On 8 December 1487 he reached modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a>. The ships then turned southwest, away from land; historians debate whether a storm drove them or they sought better winds. After 30 days at sea, on 4 February 1488, they reached the southern cape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> at what became Mossel Bay.</p><p>They continued east until 12 March 1488, when they anchored at Kwaaihoek. The crew were restless and the ships battered, and the officers unanimously urged a return, which Dias accepted. In May 1488 he first saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>; tradition says he named it the Cape of Storms and King John II renamed it. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in December 1488 after 16 months. Almost all maritime records were destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a>, and much comes from João de Barros, writing about sixty years later. His reception at court was muted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dias contributed to the design and construction of the São Gabriel and São Rafael used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>, and accompanied da Gama's fleet to the Cape Verde Islands in 1497. In 1500 he was a captain on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a>'s expedition, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> on 22 April 1500. He died on 29 May 1500 when four ships, his among them, were lost in a storm off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in 1576.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartolomeu Dias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator its sailors settled Madeira and the Azores, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until <b>Bartolomeu Dias</b> rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under João II, <b>Bartolomeu Dias</b> rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488 and showed that an oceanic route to Asia was possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Algarve. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.36,18.47&amp;year=1482" aria-label="Cape of Good Hope on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5020 3408 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bartolomeu Dias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5470 3678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5470 3678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.36,18.47&amp;year=1482">What was here in 1482? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
